[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Migrations, Interceptors, and Database Seed Strategies in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Managing database schema evolution, auditing data changes, and populating initial datasets are crucial aspects of enterprise .NET development using Entity Framework Core (EF Core). This tutorial explores how to effectively combine migrations, command interceptors, and robust seeding strategies."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Advanced Entity Framework Core Migrations"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Migrations allow you to incrementally evolve your database schema to match your application's data model. Always ensure that migrations are generated cleanly and reviewed before applying them to production environments."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate --project DataAccess --startup-project WebApi
dotnet ef database update --project DataAccess --startup-project WebApi"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Database Interceptors"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Interceptors in EF Core enable you to intercept low-level database operations. They are extremely useful for cross-cutting concerns such as automatic auditing, query performance logging, or modifying SQL commands on the fly."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class AuditingInterceptor : SaveChangesInterceptor
{
    public override ValueTask<InterceptionResult<int>> SavingChangesAsync(
        DbContextEventData eventData,
        InterceptionResult<int> result,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var context = eventData.Context;
        if (context == null) return base.SavingChangesAsync(eventData, result, cancellationToken);

        var entries = context.ChangeTracker.Entries<IAuditable>();
        foreach (var entry in entries)
        {
            if (entry.State == EntityState.Added)
            {
                entry.Entity.CreatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;
            }
            if (entry.State == EntityState.Modified)
            {
                entry.Entity.UpdatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow;
            }
        }

        return base.SavingChangesAsync(eventData, result, cancellationToken);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Robust Database Seed Strategies"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Seeding data ensures that your application has the necessary baseline data (such as roles, lookup values, or default admin accounts) upon deployment. You can implement this cleanly inside your DbContext configuration or via dedicated initialization services."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public static class ModelBuilderExtensions
{
    public static void Seed(this ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
    {
        modelBuilder.Entity<Role>().HasData(
            new Role { Id = 1, Name = "Admin" },
            new Role { Id = 2, Name = "User" }
        );
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Production"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Never run automatic migrations on startup in high-availability multi-instance environments to prevent race conditions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Register interceptors as scoped services in your Dependency Injection container when they depend on scoped services like current user context."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep seed data idempotent to prevent primary key constraint violations on subsequent application startups."}]
